Yes. There’s a real structure underneath that intuition, even if we keep the physics claims separate from the metaphor.

What you’re describing is an epistemic resolution gradient around the present. Near “now,” information is dense: direct observation, living witnesses, raw records, context, competing accounts, high-dimensional detail. Move far enough backward and the record gets thinner, noisier, selectively preserved, translated, mythologized, and compressed. Move forward and the same thing happens for a different reason: there are fewer observations because the events have not happened yet, so possibility branches dominate.

So the geometry is something like:

distant past ← low resolution ← recent past ← NOW → near future → low resolution → distant future

But the two sides are not identical.

The past is underdetermined because evidence has been lost.
The future is underdetermined because multiple outcomes remain possible.

Yet from the standpoint of a person standing at the present, both can become story-space.

That’s the part I think you’re seeing clearly: at sufficient temporal distance, history and prediction begin to use the same machinery. Both depend on models, compression, inference, archetypes, selected evidence, and narrative reconstruction.

A king from 3,000 years ago and a civilization 3,000 years from now can both end up represented to us through roughly the same cognitive form:

a few names + a few events + symbols + causal story + enormous missing volume.

The raw worlds were or will be vastly richer than the representation.

Your Doppler analogy works especially well if you treat resolution rather than frequency as the variable. I’d call it something like a Temporal Resolution Cone:

As temporal distance |\Delta t| increases, accessible detail generally falls, uncertainty rises, and representation shifts from event-level data toward compressed narrative.

Something like:

R(t) \propto \frac{I(t)}{1 + |\Delta t|}

not as literal physics, but as an intuition: available information density I decreases with distance from the observer’s present.

And recursive hindsight changes the shape of the cone. New evidence discovered today can suddenly increase resolution around some old event. Archaeology, recovered archives, DNA, astronomy, digitized records—each can sharpen a region of the past that had gone blurry.

Likewise, models can sharpen portions of the future. A solar eclipse fifty years from now may be predicted with much higher resolution than what someone will eat for breakfast next Tuesday. So temporal distance alone doesn’t determine resolution; causal constraint and surviving information matter too.

That gives you a better equation:

temporal resolution = distance × evidence × causal constraint × model quality

The philosophical consequence is interesting: “past,” “present,” and “future” are partly categories of access, not merely locations on a timeline.

And legacy sits exactly at that boundary. A human life begins as an absurdly high-dimensional stream of experience and eventually gets compressed:

person → memories → anecdotes → documents → biography → legend → name → symbol.

That is almost lossy compression through time.

Which means preserving legacy is fundamentally an information problem: how much dimensionality can you transmit across temporal distance before the person collapses into a story?

And the inverse is true for the future: how much structure can you project forward before possibility collapses into fantasy?

So your past/future symmetry isn’t that they are physically identical. It is that far enough from the present, both become reconstruction problems.

Near time is lived.

Far time is inferred.